# Cameras for a Manchester run with the wind blowing E across the full 2 km box
# (demonstrator/urban -geom geom/mcr -bearing 270 -src 30 230 1).
#
# The built-in defaults in make_urban_anim.sh were framed for a plume on a
# different heading, and two of the three panels are wrong for this one in ways
# that are obvious once rendered and invisible before:
#
# THE PLAN VIEW CROPPED THE DOMAIN. vol_urban's -fov is the VERTICAL angle and
# its up vector is derived (cross(right, fwd)), so a panel cannot be rolled --
# only moved. At 3216 m from the focus, fov 30 covers 1724 m vertically against
# a 2000 m domain, so the plume ran off both ends of the panel. Worse, the old
# camera sat offset along -x, which maps the domain's x-axis to the image's
# VERTICAL axis: a plume running W->E therefore ran up the short side of a
# 750x510 landscape panel. Rotating the offset 90 degrees about the focus
# ((x,y) -> (-y,x), same distance, same elevation) puts the domain x-axis on the
# image's horizontal axis, so the plume lies along the panel's long side, and
# fov 32 then frames it with the city legible either side.
#
# THE LOW OBLIQUE LOOKED DOWN THE PLUME. Its caption claims the plume lifts into
# faster air as it runs, and that is a statement about a VERTICAL profile, which
# the default south-west oblique shows at an angle. Moved broadside -- due south
# of the focus, low -- so the lift is seen in profile. The focus is dropped to
# 90 m, a little under the 121.6 m tallest column, to keep the horizon out of
# the upper third.
#
# The skyline sweep is unchanged: it was already framed for the full 2 km box
# and reads correctly with the plume crossing it right to left.

FOC=997.5,997.5,107.49

CAM_H=-133.0,3748.9,1438.4        # skyline sweep, unchanged
FOV_H=34

CAM_E=997.5,-1100.0,900.0         # broadside from the south, low
FOC_E=997.5,997.5,90.0
FOV_E=34

CAM_G=1054.77,-405.56,3000.10     # plan view, rotated 90 deg about the focus
FOV_G=32
